on. I'm noting the shift from singular to plural references. I am concentrating on line 16-17, understanding `ia-ti` as \"Me,\" `a-na ki-ma ma-an-ni-im` as \"like whom,\" and `tu-szi-im-ma` as an uncertain verb. Further, I am focusing on translating lines 19 and 20.\n\n\n**Interpreting the Subtleties**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on interpreting the implications of \"who acted thus?\" and considering its function as a question. I've rephrased \"a-na ma-<la> ka-as-pi-im\" to \"for whatever <amount of> silver,\" adding explanatory notes on the scribal omission and ATF notation. I am also identifying \"wa-ri-im\" as a variant of \"werîm.\"\n\n\n**Analyzing the Requirements**\n\nI'm now integrating the provided ATF line jumps into the translation, ensuring clear cross-referencing to the original text. I'm focusing on \"nasihtum,\" translating it literally and adding a note about the debated nuances. The D-stem preterite of `kullum` in line 20 is being considered for the final translation.\n\n\n**Refining the Structure**\n\nI am now focusing on restructuring the translation to mirror the ATF layout precisely. I am adding the \"Obverse,\" \"Reverse,\" and \"Left Edge\" labels, as well as line numbers from the ATF transliteration. I'm focusing on crafting an easily readable, line-by-line translation that accurately reflects the original tablet. I'm still working on \"i-na a-li-ik te-el-mu-un,\" and how to integrate it.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Translation**\n\nI am now focusing on the final lines, ensuring each phrase carries the correct meaning. I have translated the \"If you (pl.) will take (them), take (them)!\" line and understand the sender's indignation. I've integrated all the line jumps. I am refining the phrases of the final lines to make sure the meaning is perfectly clear. I have completed the final translation and am preparing for a final review.\n\n\n**Revising Bracket Usage**\n\nI am now clarifying my bracketing strategy for damaged text, deciding whether to include `